@lesson rl-foundations
Q: With rewards \(r_1=1, r_2=1, r_3=1\) and \(\gamma=0.9\), what is the return \(G_0\)?
= 2.71 ±0.005
> \(1+0.9+0.81=2.71\).

Q: What is the term in brackets in the Q-learning update \(Q\leftarrow Q+\alpha[r+\gamma\max_{a'}Q(s',a')-Q]\)?
* The temporal-difference error
- The policy gradient
- The advantage of the best action
- The learning rate
> It measures how much better or worse the outcome was than predicted.

Q: Why do agents use ε-greedy action selection?
* To keep exploring actions that may be better than the current best estimate
- To make training deterministic
- To reduce memory
- To avoid rewards
> Without exploration, early estimates can lock the agent into a poor policy.

Q: Value iteration requires a known model of the environment's transitions and rewards.
= true
> It sweeps the Bellman optimality equation over all states; Q-learning learns from samples instead.

@lesson policy-gradient
Q: Why do environment transition probabilities drop out of the policy gradient?
* They do not depend on the policy parameters \(\theta\)
- They are always 1
- They are estimated separately
- Because rewards are zero
> Only the \(\log\pi_\theta(a_t\mid s_t)\) terms of the trajectory log-probability depend on \(\theta\).

Q: What does subtracting a baseline \(V(s_t)\) from the return do?
* Reduces variance while keeping the gradient unbiased
- Makes the gradient biased but faster
- Removes exploration
- Doubles the learning rate
> The advantage \(G_t-V(s_t)\) asks how much better than expected an action was.

Q: What does PPO's clipping prevent?
- Exploration
* Policy updates that move the probability ratio too far in one step
- Negative rewards
- Using a value function
> Clipping \(r_t(\theta)\) to \([1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon]\) keeps updates conservative.

@lesson deep-rl
Q: Which two tricks stabilized DQN? (select all)
* An experience replay buffer
* A slowly updated target network
- A causal mask
- Dropout on the Q-values
> Replay breaks correlations; the target network stops the regression target from chasing itself.

Q: Why does behaviour cloning suffer from compounding errors?
* Small mistakes lead to states the expert never visited, where the policy is worse
- The expert is always wrong
- It has no loss function
- It uses rewards
> DAgger fixes this by labelling the states the learner actually reaches.

Q: In MCTS-based systems like AlphaZero, what guides which moves to explore?
* A neural network's prior and value estimates combined with visit counts
- Random choice only
- Human annotations
- The final score only
> The upper-confidence rule balances promising moves and under-explored ones.

@lesson exam
Q: In RLHF, which part plays the role of the environment's reward?
* A reward model (or verifier), with a KL penalty to the reference policy
- The tokenizer
- The pretraining loss
- The learning-rate scheduler
> The policy is the LLM, actions are tokens, and the episode is a full response.

Q: An agent learns to exploit a bug in the simulator for huge reward instead of doing the task. What is this called?
- Overfitting
* Reward hacking (specification gaming)
- Mode collapse
- Covariate shift
> The agent optimizes the reward as written, not as intended.

Q: Which method can learn from a fixed dataset without any environment interaction? (select all)
* Behaviour cloning
* Offline RL with conservative value estimates
- On-policy PPO
- Online ε-greedy Q-learning
> On-policy and online methods must collect fresh experience.

Q: A discount factor of \(\gamma=0\) makes the agent care only about the immediate reward.
= true
> All future rewards are multiplied by zero.
